"Charging by Induction: teaching notes",
"Charging by Induction",
"In a conductor, electrons move freely. A charged object nearby pulls some toward it and leaves the far side with the opposite charge (polarization), with no change in total charge.",
"Separate the near and far parts (or let charge flow to ground) while the charged object is still there, and the charge stays: charging by induction, without touching.",
"A grounded conductor is held at zero potential; charge flows to or from the Earth to keep it there.",
"Conductors in contact share charge until they are at one potential.",
"Use the step bar (<strong>Next step</strong>).",
"<strong>Charging two spheres by induction.</strong> Touching spheres, then a +{#} nC rod near A: A's near side goes negative, B's far side positive, total zero. Separate them: A keeps −{#} nC and B +{#} nC. Remove the rod: the charges stay and spread out.",
"<strong>Charging one sphere by grounding.</strong> The rod {#} cm from A; ground A: −{#} nC flows up from the Earth. Remove the ground, then the rod: A stays negative. Ask Q3 about the order.",
"<strong>Sharing charge by contact.</strong> +{#} nC on a {#} cm sphere touches a neutral {#} cm sphere: {#} nC and {#} nC.",
